<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The mark, on the adaptive icon's 108dp canvas.

  These are the five shapes from public/logo.svg, unchanged: the same 42
  corners, every edge straight. What was here before was the C traced from the
  old product's artwork, which stopped being this product's logo when the site
  did and left the launcher showing a brand nobody else uses any more.

  The group fits them into 72dp of the 108dp canvas, which is the safe zone
  every launcher mask keeps: a circle, a squircle and a rounded square each cut
  somewhere different outside it, and a mark that reaches past it loses a
  different piece of itself on every phone. The source shapes span 84.4 x 100
  from (7.8, 0), so 0.72 scales the taller side to 72 and the offsets put the
  result's centre on the canvas centre.

  No wordmark. "SYX VPN" sits under the mark on the site, where it is read at
  120px; at 48dp in a launcher it is a grey smear, and the app's name is
  already written under the icon by the launcher itself.

  The same shapes are in public/logo.svg. Changing one without the other is how
  a brand ends up with two logos.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="108dp"
    android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108"
    android:viewportHeight="108">
    <group
        android:scaleX="0.72"
        android:scaleY="0.72"
        android:translateX="18"
        android:translateY="18">
